\section{Localize Failure Before Translating It}

An exception does more than choose a return value: it initiates
control transfer, searches for handlers, and may unwind state
owned by intervening frames. Hamilton's CLR extract describes a
shared two-pass model with exception clause tables and cleanup.
Languages targeting that runtime can participate in a common
mechanism. That is not a property of an arbitrary C boundary.
Unless the participants deliberately specify compatible unwinding,
keep C++ exceptions and host exceptions on their originating sides.
An expected failure across the boundary travels as a value.

Suppose a host requests an encoded thumbnail from a C facade to a
C++ library. The C facade writes a pointer and length only on
success, and optionally initializes a caller-allocated diagnostic
record on failure. Internal exceptions are translated by the
facade, after C++ scope cleanup. The host sees the stable status
and chooses whether to return an error value or raise its own
exception. Native error numbers are often insufficient to
distinguish domain failures, missing features and recoverable
resource problems for language callers. Preserve the native
category and raw code in an internal diagnostic even when
several statuses share a common public variant.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lp{0.61\linewidth}}
\hline
Condition & Across-boundary representation \\
\hline
Malformed image & Domain status; output pointer remains null. \\
Unsupported encoding & Capability status; retry only with other input. \\
Allocation failure & Resource status; fixed diagnostic if possible. \\
Host visitor raises & Native stop sentinel plus saved host error. \\
Unknown failure code & Generic error with original code retained. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Write Down the Status-Output Relation}

For every status, say which outputs have defined values. Suppose
\texttt{SIZE} means the output is absent but a separate required
length is valid; ordinary failure promises neither output. A
wrapper cannot call its generic result decoder on every branch
and then replace the result with an error. The read itself can
be invalid. Likewise, nonlocal transfers that skip a frame's
cleanup invalidate the reasoning about what has been released.
Replace a library's documented nonlocal exit protocol with a
shim that contains it within a compatible, carefully scoped region,
or reject the interface. Do not route such a transfer across a
host entry frame and expect its cleanup to run.

There may be secondary error state, such as a thread-local error
number. Read it only when the documented return sentinel indicates
failure, and capture it immediately at the native boundary, before
logging, deallocation or VM transitions. An unrelated value in that
slot on success is not an error. \Cref{fig:visual-392} shows
the two independent cleanup regions and the status transmitted
between them.

