\section{Translate Under Transactional Output Rules}

\begin{verbatim}
facade_encode(input, out, diagnostic):
    out.ptr := null
    out.len := 0
    clear(diagnostic)
    try:
        with native_owned_buffer(encode_cpp(input)) as buf:
            length := checked_c_size_t(buf.length)
            # Everything after release must be nonthrowing.
            ptr := buf.release_to_caller()
            out.ptr := ptr
            out.len := length
            return OK
    catch BadImage:
        write_fixed_diagnostic(diagnostic, "bad image")
        return BAD_INPUT
    catch OutOfMemory:
        write_fixed_diagnostic(diagnostic, "out of memory")
        return RESOURCE
    catch supported_unexpected_exception:
        write_fixed_diagnostic(diagnostic, "encoder failure")
        return INTERNAL

host_encode(image):
    with native_view(image) as input:
        status, out, diag := call_facade(input)
        if status == OK:
            with owned_native_result(out) as buffer:
                return copy_buffer_to_host(buffer)
    raise translate(status, diag)
\end{verbatim}

The diagnostic writer must not allocate or throw while handling
allocation failure. This example assumes callers obey the facade
contract for output pointers, and that creating the host owner
is nonthrowing until it has assumed cleanup responsibility. Put
allocator identity in the buffer owner, not the diagnostic.
A generic catch does not catch native faults such as invalid
memory accesses, and must not swallow runtime conditions that
have their own required propagation protocol. Scope the guarantee
to exceptions the implementation can handle correctly.

\section{Make the Reverse Path Obey the Same Rule}

If the encoder calls a host progress visitor, its entry stub
catches a recoverable host exception, saves it in a rooted slot,
and returns the library's stop code. The outer wrapper raises the
saved error after the native caller has actually stopped and its
foreign frame has been restored. A JNI-inspired API may instead
represent an outstanding exception as explicit runtime state;
Furr and Foster's study of JNI glue illustrates why using runtime
APIs through C requires modeling more than ordinary argument
types. For a pending-error interface, check the documented pending
state and use only the cleanup and error APIs permitted in that
state. Clearing it silently and trusting a placeholder result
changes the language semantics.

Arrange a fixture where the progress visitor raises and native
cleanup encounters its own recoverable diagnostic. Record a
precedence rule: here the first host error is primary, and later
cleanup details are secondary. Test a normal native cancellation
with no saved exception as a separate path. Finally inject an
error after a native allocation but before publication, checking
that the intermediate allocation is freed and that the host
never sees a half-filled successful result.

\section{Describe Partial Work Without Inventing Rollback}

Sometimes a native writer has already sent several records
when a failure occurs. The raw contract can expose a committed
prefix count on a separate failure status. Translate that as
an interruption with an explicit prefix, not as a generic
exception suggesting that nothing was written. The count is
valid only on success and that specific partial status, and
must not exceed the number supplied. An interface promising
an all-or-nothing write cannot implement that promise merely
by hiding the partial count. It needs a separate facility,
such as writing to a staging object that can then be published.

For a resumable writer, suppose five records were submitted
and the supplied count is two. The binding can return the
starting index of the next unwritten record, with clear units
and indexing. A retry of the full original array would write
the first two records twice. If a failure leaves the exact
commit point unknown, report that uncertainty rather than
assuming a committable prefix of zero. Use a fixture to fail
before the first record, between two records, and after all
records are written but before acknowledgement. Those cases
exercise different host policies even when a library uses the
same low-level error number for all of them.
